  "slug": "peripheral-single-cell-9588",
  "summary": "Peripheral and single-cell immune readouts are being tested as dynamic biomarkers of checkpoint and cell therapy response across cancer types.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the dissection of CD8+ T-cell states, exhaustion, and clonal architecture during immunotherapy, with single-cell and peripheral readouts used to resolve them. In AML, scRNA-seq and TCR profiling on HMA-venetoclax regimens link CD8+ T-cell expansion and clonal diversity to response, and single-cell work separates self-derived from donor-derived exhaustion niches with distinct NFAT-linked transcriptional features. Peripheral studies extend this. Longitudinal flow cytometry in ESCC patients on anti-PD-1 neoadjuvant therapy tracks PD-1+ CD8 T and gamma-delta T features, and a CD68+ tissue-homing CD8 subset in blood tracks lung cancer anti-PD-1 response. Murine work shows chemotherapy added to radiotherapy drives TPEX-biased clonal CD8 expansion, and CAR-T studies connect cystine restriction to reduced exhaustion. What is new is the framing of circulating T-cell subsets as early dynamic biomarkers. Several items, including reviews and macrophage or vaccine studies, address adjacent questions.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-16"
}
